__INLINE:schedule.ts__
// Where a tick's claims become the tick's plan. Three steps, in this order: the
// placeholders a claimless branch sends are dropped, what is left is bounded by
// AR_DISPATCH_BATCH_CAP, and every unit that survives is given the id of the
// workflow its kind asks for. The whole module behind the marker above is
// spliced in, so clampIntervalSeconds arrives beside capBatch and goes unused
// here, the reschedule it states being SQL already, inside the claims.
//
// The drop is keyed on the ABSENT unit_kind rather than on which branch an item
// came in on, because the Merge node in front is where a branch stops being
// readable: past it an item is an item, and the only handle left is what a
// claim put IN the row. A Postgres node whose statement is not a SELECT emits
// one success item when it returns no rows, so a tick with nothing due anywhere
// arrives here as two items, and a half-empty one as real rows plus a
// placeholder. No placeholder carries a unit_kind and every claimed row does.
//
// Dropping before capping is why these are two steps rather than one filtered
// slice. A placeholder counted toward the cap spends a slot on a row that does
// not exist, so a tick with one branch idle would dispatch one fewer than the
// operator set, and nothing about a short pass says why.
//
// Routing runs last and, unlike the two steps in front of it, its position is
// free. Dropping before capping decides what gets dispatched; routing before or
// after the cap decides nothing, a unit resolving the same id whether the cap
// keeps it or drops it. It is written at the end so the item this node emits is
// built in one place, not because the other order would break anything.
//
// This is the SECOND place the cap is applied and it is not a re-check of an
// already-bounded batch. Each claim carries the same setting as a SQL LIMIT, so
// a LIMIT bounds a BRANCH and this call bounds the TICK: a pass finding both
// tables backlogged claims twice the cap and dispatches the cap. What it drops
// was already CLAIMED, the claim having moved next_run_at before anything
// looked at the row, so a dropped row waits a whole interval rather than being
// taken by the tick after this one. capBatch in src/lib/schedule.ts carries the
// rest, including why a LIMIT one edit away from being tuned off is not the
// copy to rely on.
//
// The settings are quoted and parsed rather than spliced in bare. A marker
// resolves to whatever the settings chain answered, and quoting is what keeps
// every possible answer a valid literal: an unparseable cap becomes NaN and
// comes back as capBatch's own refusal naming the value, where a bare marker
// would leave a ReferenceError naming nothing. The two workflow ids take the
// first half of that and not the second. Quoting is what keeps them literals
// too, an id spliced in bare being a pair of names the node would die on, but
// there is no parse for them to fail: whatever the chain answered becomes the
// id, and an id that is wrong is wrong on the instance rather than here.
//
// What arrives here is NOT ordered by how overdue a row is. A claim's ORDER BY
// picks WHICH rows it takes and never the order they come back in, and the
// merge concatenates one branch after the other whatever either returned. So
// the cap takes the front of an unordered stream: what it drops is arbitrary
// among the rows claimed, not the least overdue among them.
//
// Arbitrary is not the same as random: the merge puts input 0 first, so every
// topics claim precedes every export claim. A topics table backlogged past the
// cap on its own therefore leaves no slot for an export subscription at all,
// and those rows wait a whole interval on every tick it stays that way.
//
// What a unit is routed TO is settled by its kind and by nothing else on the
// row, which the claims already argue: unit_kind is a literal precisely because
// a claimed row says nothing about the table it came from. What only this node
// can say is WHEN that pairing is settled. Both ids arrive as markers, so which
// workflow a kind reaches is written into the artifact at build time and is not
// something the instance holds. Retargeting a dispatch is an edit to
// ENV_DEFAULTS or to a deploy environment followed by a rebuild, never a row an
// operator updates, and a workflow renamed on an instance leaves this node
// addressing the old id until something builds again.
//
// TARGET_BY_KIND is a Map and not an object literal, and the difference is what
// a kind with no entry gets back. A plain object answers for names nothing put
// in it: constructor, __proto__, toString and valueOf all come back as
// inherited members, so a kind spelled any of those would resolve to a function
// or to an object where an id belongs, and whatever invoked it would be handed
// that. Map.get answers undefined for every name that was never set.
//
// A kind with no entry is passed on carrying a null target rather than dropped
// or raised, and both of those are worse. Dropping it would strand a row that
// was CLAIMED and rescheduled before anything looked at it, so it would wait a
// whole interval with nothing anywhere recording that it went nowhere. Raising
// would take the tick down, and every other row in that tick was rescheduled
// too, so one unroutable unit would cost the whole pass. Passed on, it carries
// a null where an id belongs, fails at the node that invokes it, and closes its
// run on the error branch, which is the same route a correct id with no
// workflow behind it takes. That node is Invoke Target Workflow, behind the
// run-opening node, and Close Run Failed sits on the output it takes when an
// invocation fails. The route is shared and the record is not: Close Run Failed
// writes the target into the run's errors as it stood, so a JSON null there
// names a kind this node had no entry for where a string names an id that had
// nothing behind it.
//
// The target is written as null rather than left off the item, because those
// two are not one value on both sides of a JSON round trip: an undefined member
// is a member here and gone once the item is written out, so which of them a
// reader saw would depend on where they stood. It is spelled
// target_workflow_id, snake_case like the members a claim wrote, so a reader
// past this node sees one shape rather than two, and it is the one member on a
// claimed unit that no claim wrote. The unit is spread into a new object rather
// than written to: the merge passes items on by reference, so a claimed row is
// still the input item's own json, and setting a member on it would write into
// what this node was handed.
const CAP = Number('__ENVVAR:AR_DISPATCH_BATCH_CAP__');
const TARGET_BY_KIND = new Map([
  ['topic', '__ENVVAR:AR_TOPIC_WORKFLOW_ID__'],
  ['export', '__ENVVAR:AR_EXPORT_WORKFLOW_ID__'],
]);
const claimed = $input.all().map((i) => i.json).filter((r) => r && r.unit_kind != null);
return capBatch(claimed, CAP).map((unit) => ({
  json: { ...unit, target_workflow_id: TARGET_BY_KIND.get(unit.unit_kind) ?? null },
}));
